=== Branch Management
tag::reference[]

(((branches, management)))
Now that Nora and Sam are creating, switching, and merging branches, Nora needs a way to keep everything organized. The `git branch` command does more than just create branches; it’s also her primary tool for managing them.

==== Listing Your Branches
tag::procedure[]

If Nora runs `git branch` without any arguments, she gets a simple list of her current branches:

[source,bash]
----
$ git branch
* main
  option/card-layout
  option/list-layout
  option/canvas-freeform
----

The asterisk (`*`) indicates the branch Nora currently has checked out (where **HEAD** is pointing).

==== Viewing Branch Details
tag::procedure[]

To see the last checkpoint (commit) on each branch, she can use the `-v` flag:

[source,bash]
----
$ git branch -v
* main                   f30ab [Research: Product] Add parallel UI goals
  option/card-layout     34ac2 [Design: Results] Add initial grid layout
  option/list-layout     98ca9 [Design: Results] Prototype vertical stack
----

==== Filtering Merged and Unmerged Branches
tag::procedure[]

As the SketchSpark repo grows, Nora will want to know which branches have already been integrated into `main` and which are still work-in-progress.

To see which branches she has already merged into her current branch, she runs:

[source,bash]
----
$ git branch --merged
  option/card-layout
* main
----

Since `option/card-layout` is in this list, Nora knows she can safely delete it without losing any work.

To see all the branches that contain work she *hasn't* merged yet:

[source,bash]
----
$ git branch --no-merged
  option/list-layout
  option/canvas-freeform
----

This is Nora's "to-do" list. It shows her exactly which explorations are still active and haven't been reconciled with the main project yet.

==== Deleting a Branch
tag::procedure[]

(((git commands, branch)))
Once an exploration is finished or a fix is merged, Nora should delete the branch to keep the repo clean:

[source,bash]
----
$ git branch -d option/card-layout
----

If Nora tries to delete a branch that *isn't* merged yet (like `option/list-layout`), Git will warn her and protect her work:

[source,bash]
----
$ git branch -d option/list-layout
error: The branch 'option/list-layout' is not fully merged.
If you are sure you want to delete it, run 'git branch -D option/list-layout'.
----

If Nora is absolutely sure she wants to discard the "list layout" experiment entirely, she can use the capital `-D` flag to force the deletion.

==== Renaming a Branch
tag::procedure[]

If Nora wants to rename a branch—for example, to make it more descriptive—she can use the `-m` flag:

[source,bash]
----
$ git branch -m option/card-layout option/results-grid
----

This renames the local branch. If the branch has already been pushed to a server, there are a few more steps to synchronize that change, which we’ll cover in <<_remote_branches>>.
